halk or grip attachments only when you need them."}}]}</script>`}>
<div class="article-body"><div class="callout"><h2 id="glance" style="margin-top:0">At a glance</h2><ol class="stop-steps"><li><strong>Tested here:</strong> nothing. DeadHangs.com has not load-tested any equipment.</li><li><strong>What guides compare:</strong> mounting type, fit checks, rated load and manufacturer instructions.</li><li><strong>Affiliate links:</strong> labeled, and they open Amazon search results.</li><li><strong>Your check:</strong> read the exact product's documentation before ordering.</li></ol><p>A future hands-on review must name the model, test date, setup and limitations.</p></div><h2>Where should you start by mounting type?</h2><p>Start with the guide that matches your space: the equipment guide for the category comparison, then the doorway, wall-mounted or pull-up bar guide for fit checks. Chalk and grip attachment guides come after you have a bar.</p><p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Equipment guides by need" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Your situation</th><th scope="col">Guide</th><th scope="col">What it checks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Choosing a mounting type</td><td><a href="/equipment-guide/">Dead hang equipment guide</a></td><td>Category comparison and buying checks</td></tr><tr><td>Comparing every bar type</td><td><a href="/best-pullup-bars/">Pull-up bar buyer's guide</a></td><td>Mounting, fit, load rating and diameter</td></tr><tr><td>Renting, no drilling</td><td><a href="/doorway-bars/">Door frame pull-up bar guide</a></td><td>Opening width, trim, frame and load rating</td></tr><tr><td>Owning a wall with studs</td><td><a href="/wall-mounted-bars/">Wall-mounted bar guide</a></td><td>Studs, hardware, depth and load rating</td></tr><tr><td>Sweaty hands</td><td><a href="/best-chalk/">Chalk guide</a></td><td>Liquid, block and alternatives</td></tr><tr><td>Harder grip work</td><td><a href="/grip-attachments/">Grip attachments guide</a></td><td>Fat grips, fingerboards, rings and grippers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>What does a useful equipment review need?</h2><p>A useful equipment review explains who the product fits and what must be checked before buying. Mounting requirements, available space and the manufacturer's instructions come before a recommendation.</p><ul><li>Identify the exact model and the current manufacturer documentation.</li><li>State the mounting surface, doorway measurements or floor space required.</li><li>Separate manufacturer specifications from observations made during a documented test.</li><li>Describe limitations and the likely reason to choose another category.</li><li>Link the reader to current availability rather than presenting an undated price as current.</li></ul><h2>Has DeadHangs.com tested this equipment?</h2><p>DeadHangs.com has not load-tested any equipment. Its guides are research-based buying guides.</p><p>A future hands-on review must identify the model, test date, setup and actual observations. It must also explain any limitations. We will not award a verified load rating from an informal home test.</p><h2>Choose the mounting category first</h2><p>A doorway bar, fixed wall bar and free-standing station solve different space problems. Installation requirements vary between products in the same category. Read the exact manufacturer's instructions before buying or fitting one.</p><p>Start with the <a href="/equipment-guide/">dead hang equipment guide</a> for the category comparison and buying checks.</p><h2>Doorframe bars</h2><p>A doorframe bar depends on the door trim, the frame material and the bar's rated load. Check all three before ordering.</p><p>The <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorframe bar guide</a> lists the checks in order.</p><h2>Wall-mounted bars</h2><p>A wall-mounted bar depends on the wall structure, the hardware and the bar's rated load. Check the studs and the manufacturer's fixings before drilling.</p><p>The <a href="/wall-mounted-bars/">wall-mounted bar guide</a> covers installation checks.</p><h2>Are the Amazon links affiliate links?</h2><p>Yes. DeadHangs.com is an Amazon Associate and earns from qualifying purchases. Each guide labels these links as affiliate links, and they open Amazon search results. An affiliate relationship does not prove a product has been tested here.</p><p>See our <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a> for source and disclosure rules.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><h3>Does DeadHangs.com test the pull-up bars it links to?</h3><p>No. DeadHangs.com has not load-tested any equipment. Its guides are research-based buying guides that compare mounting types, fit checks and manufacturer specifications. A future hands-on review would name the model, test date, setup and limitations.</p></div><div class="faq"><h3>Are the Amazon links on DeadHangs.com affiliate links?</h3><p>Yes. DeadHangs.com is an Amazon Associate and earns from qualifying purchases. Each link is labeled as an affiliate link and opens Amazon search results, not a single product. An affiliate link does not mean the product has been tested here.</p></div><div class="faq"><h3>How do I check a pull-up bar's load rating?</h3><p>Use the manufacturer's rated load in the exact product's documentation and stay well under the maximum. DeadHangs.com does not verify load ratings with home tests, and a rating for one model does not carry over to another.</p></div><div class="faq"><h3>Which equipment guide should I read first?</h3><p>Start with the dead hang equipment guide to choose a mounting type for your space. Then read the doorway bar guide or the wall-mounted bar guide, and add chalk or grip attachments only when you need them.</p></div><h2>Report a specification change</h2><p>Include the product name and the manufacturer's current documentation when reporting a correction. We can then compare the claim with the source.</p><p>Use the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> to send feedback.</p></div>
